{%- set churchIntro -%}<p class="page-intro">St Peter's in Rous Lench, a few miles north-west of Harvington, is the R in the ARCH Benefice. It is a Grade I listed Norman church, best known for its carved doorway and the monuments of the Rous family, who gave the village its name.</p>{%- endset -%}
{%- set churchAbout -%}<h2>The church through the centuries</h2>
    <p>St Peter's was built around the middle of the 12th century, possibly where a church had stood since the 9th. Its Norman nave opens into the north aisle through three round arches with scalloped capitals, and the chancel arch is of the same age.</p>
    <p>Between 1884 and 1896 Frederick Preedy, the architect who also restored St James in Harvington, reworked the chancel and north aisle and built the north chapel in a round-arched style. He added the rose window and the little bellcote, which took the place of a wooden tower.</p>
    <h2>Things to see</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The Norman south doorway, with zig-zag carving and twisted columns, and the figure of Christ above it, seated and giving a blessing.</li>
      <li>The Rous family memorials, gathered into their own chapel in 1886. The grandest is Edward Rous's tomb of 1611, its canopy topped with obelisks.</li>
      <li>Not one but two 17th-century pulpits.</li>
      <li>Two unhung bells: one cast around 1600 with the words "GOD BE OUR SPEDE", and one of 1661.</li>
    </ul>{%- endset -%}
{%- set servicesIntro -%}<p>St Peter's has services on some Sundays, taking turns with the other ARCH churches. Coming up:</p>{%- endset -%}
